    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The issue you're experiencing now is likely due to the role you're using to run the Lambda function. S3 can be very specific about permissions, and it may not be granting the necessary permissions for ARNs of objects in the bucket (e.g., logs). \n\nTrying adding an additional permission to the statement, allowing the role to get bucket ACLs, which you can do with the following action: \n```\n\"Action\": \"s3:GetObjectAcl\"\n```\nHowever, this would still not work since s3.amazonaws.com has full access to your bucket.\n\nYou need to ensure that the IAM role you're using to run the Lambda function has the necessary permissions to `сman` (get the owner of) the S3 bucket, as well as any specific permissions your application needs. \n\nHere is an updated policy:\n\n```\n{\n  \"Version\": \"2012-10-17\",\n  \"Statement\": [\n    {\n      \"Effect\": \"Allow\",\n      \"Action\": [\n        \"s3:GetObjectAcl\",\n        \"logs:CreateLogGroup\",\n        \"logs:CreateLogStream\",\n        \"logs:PutLogEvents\"\n      ],\n      \"Resource\": [\n        \"arn:aws:s3",
